{"about":"0f7a8b30a80188d2b98cfe5cccc160f1b2c70b259fbc2ab374c714f493c4a6bd","body":"The conjecture assumes that rejecting induction necessitates the loss of all 'comfort' regarding probability, yet it fails to distinguish between logical probability (which Popper rejects as applicable to universal laws) and subjective degrees of belief or pragmatic reliability. By framing the cost as a total absence of measurable progress, the objection overlooks that Popper's framework offers a non-probabilistic metric of progress: the increase in empirical content and the severity of tests passed. The 'comfort' lost is merely the illusion of inductive validation, not the ability to rationally prefer one theory over another based on its surviving critical scrutiny.","kind":"mini.criticism.v1","named":"The conflation of probabilistic certainty with rational preference"}